WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.160
Hi, this is Robert Sheer with another edition of Sheer Intelligence and Intelligence comes

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.120
some of my guests. Radio station gave me the title of the show I know is South

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:36.400
Zaregon and I've had this guest on. I think this is our third encounter, maybe more.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:43.640
John Curriaco who I first got to know when he was in the news as the person

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.880
who was blue to whistle really. We could talk about the details of that on

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.180
on the torture program, the great shame of America,

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.440
some could argue maybe the greatest shame

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.780
in the last half century, certainly up there,

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.060
and that still haunts the reputation of the country

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.380
and really makes this question what we really are all about.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.780
And we'll get into his case a little bit.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.620
We've done that before.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.700
And let's just put it this way.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.200
He is the only person connected with the torture program,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.700
and he didn't do any of it.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.200
He was not involved in the torture.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.900
He refused to be trained in it.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.100
He was very successful in the arrest of, at the time,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.000
what was thought to be the third ranking leader of al-Qaeda.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.900
And he and an FBI agent got all of the,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.400
received all of the usable information ever obtained

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:49.920
for Mava Vizeda. He's now still in Guantanamo, still has never had a trial, and has been tortured

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.120
extensively, of course, along with a lot of the prisoners, all of the prisoners that are there.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:03.280
And yet no one connected with the program at the CIA or the psychologists or rather never

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:11.600
who have been accused of a crime, and John Kriakou served over two years in federal prison.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.920
We'll get into that issue, and maybe talk a little bit about the Julian Assange case

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.640
and how we treat whistleblowers.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:27.240
But the reason I wanted to do this interview today is that John was speaking on my class,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.800
where I teach at USC, and he told a story about what has happened to him, because

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.520
He lost, obviously he had left the CIA,

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.240
but he lost his pension and he was led to a divorce

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.640
because his wife was also at the CIA.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.520
And he's been in battles about status

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.840
and custody battles were more appropriate to this story

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.560
with his wife.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.600
And as recently as last August,

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:07.680
August 22, still pretty recent, during the custody hearing, and I'll let John tell the story,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:18.400
it was revealed that his wife had been in fact spying on him for years and including

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:25.040
preceding the birth of the last two of their five children. And I thought, my God, this is a

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:32.800
story you get out of a totalitarian regime out of Stalin's Russia or Nazi Germany or something,

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.440
what is this? So why don't you tell that story so I don't mangle it?

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:47.120
Yeah, it's a terrible story really. My ex-wife and I were both senior CIA officers and

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.400
as you might imagine, as you might assume, my arrest after blowing the whistle on the torture

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:06.000
program was a great blow to our marriage. About a year and a half after I got home from prison,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:13.120
she began a relationship with someone at work. I caught them and I filed for divorce.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.840
We fought for custody for years. We continue to fight over custody, but we were in court

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:27.520
over custody in August of 2022 and I was on the stand and my attorney asked me a very simple question.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:36.640
He said, was she a good wife? And I said, oh, she was an amazing wife. She stuck by me like a rock

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:43.440
when I got in trouble. She toughed out my two years in prison. She took care of the kids and

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.280
and the household, she was great.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.120
In fact, I said, she was so supportive

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.600
that she went with me to ABC News

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.000
on the day that I blew the whistle

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.480
on the CIA's torture program.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.680
She sat just off camera and when I finished,

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.400
I said, how did I do?

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.760
She said, great.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.960
I said, I didn't say anything classified, right?

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.780
And she said, no, you did great.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.120
Well, I finished my testimony

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.960
And she went up on the stand and her attorney said,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.440
is it true that you went with him to ABC News

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.020
when he blew the whistle on the CIA's torture program?

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.660
She said, yes.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.520
The attorney said, why did you do that?

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.560
And she answered, the CIA's Office of Security

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.040
instructed me to go with him

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.580
to report back to them on his activities.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.520
I was dumbstruck when she said it.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.160
My lawyer actually kicked me under the table.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.120
And afterwards, my lawyer said,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.720
do you think she said that just to hurt you?

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.340
Or did she mean it?

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.840
And I said, you know, after thinking about it

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.280
for a little while,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.920
about six months after I blew the whistle

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.520
on the torture program,

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.880
the Los Angeles Times asked me to write an op-ed

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.960
on Iranian foreign policy in Latin America.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.280
And I had sat down at my computer

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.480
to start writing this piece and my phone rang

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.140
and it was the CIA general counsel's office.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.160
And they said, you better not be writing about Iran today.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.240
And I said, I don't work for you guys anymore.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:22.580
You don't get to say what I write and what I don't write.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.580
Besides, I get everything cleared and I hung up on them.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.440
And I went to my wife and I said,

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.720
can you believe these guys had the nerve

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.480
to call me and threaten me

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.440
and tell me not to write about Iran?

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.020
But how could they know I was writing about Iran?

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:41.580
And she said, they must have somebody at the LA Times.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:46.240
And I said, oh my God, that would be so illegal,

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.000
but they must, they must have somebody at the LA Times.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:54.000
Well, coming out of that courtroom in August of 2022,

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.700
made me realize after all those years,

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.580
14 years had passed,

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.040
they didn't have anybody at the LA Times.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:04.360
She was reporting back to them on my activities.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.760
she told them that I was writing about Iran that day.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.360
Well, now, we really don't know that for sure,

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.680
but you're just surprising that, right?

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.680
Sure, I mean, you'd have to ask too

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.560
if the CIA would wanna take the legal risk

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:26.200
of placing somebody inside the Los Angeles Times

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.200
or any other outlet.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:31.680
Of course, they could, they might, I don't know.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.600
But her admission that she was reporting back to the CIA

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.040
on my activities made it clearer for me.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.480
Well, this is amazing though in a way,

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:44.360
because what had happened was, as you say,

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.160
you were both senior officers at the CIA.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:48.000
Right.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.480
And you, and for people who don't know this story,

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.000
I've interviewed you before other people have.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.560
You've written the books about it.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:58.840
But just to do the short story here,

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:04.560
You were a naive patriotic American.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.360
You were at college at George Washington University.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.520
One of your professors was actually a recruiter for the CIA.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.280
He approached you.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.120
He asked you if you were interested.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.880
You already knew Greek, because you

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.960
come from a Greek Orthodox family.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.680
They sent you to school for a year to learn Arabic.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.400
And you went into the agency versus an analyst.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.360
and then as a covert agent.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:32.200
Correct.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:36.120
And you were made the head of the Anti-Terrorism Bureau

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.240
in Pakistan, where you were involved in the capture

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.640
above the Soviet Union and so forth.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.680
And then you were going along with the program

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.360
and arresting people and breaking into houses

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:51.920
and doing the whole thing.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.040
When torture came in, in the form

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.640
of these two psychologists who thought

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.760
They had the secret way.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:01.280
The FBI, which had been involved with you

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:04.560
in interrogating prisoners, they knew how to do it

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.480
and so forth, didn't use torture,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.000
were opposed to torture actually.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.200
You were offered, they asked you if you wanted to be trained

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.680
and you wouldn't go in for it and then your career ended.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.280
So I want to be clear about that.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:22.120
What happened was you were challenged then

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.960
on this ABC show of whether you were in fact

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:30.920
source of the torture program, whether you had tortured, right?

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:31.920
That's right.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:32.920
That's right.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:36.000
And in fact, that's the reason why I agreed to the interview in the first place.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.920
Because Brian Ross, from ABC News, Brian Ross, who's won a dozen Emmy Awards and two or

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:46.560
three George Polk Awards, the guy's a very serious investigative journalist.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.880
He said that he had a source who said that I had tortured Abu Zubeda.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.120
And I said that that was absolutely untrue.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:58.560
He responded with what I later learned was an old reporter's trick by saying, you're

00:09:58.560 --> 00:09:59.960
welcome to come on the show and defend.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.760
yourself. And so, you know, once I was able to conclude that his source was at the White House

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:16.000
and they were likely to try to blame me for the torture program, I decided to not just go on

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.680
and defend myself, but to go on and answer truthfully whatever question he might have.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.960
And then how did revealing the name come up?

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:39.800
Right. So, about six months later, I got an email from a journalist who was writing a book

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.360
on something called the Abu Omar rendition.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:49.440
Abu Omar was an Egyptian cleric living in Milan, Italy, and the CIA believed

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:52.880
that he was an Al-Qaeda plant.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.400
And so the CIA kidnapped him,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.160
just snatched him off the street

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.760
while he was walking down the street in Milan one morning,

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:04.960
rendered him to Egypt, tortured him,

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:08.240
and it turned out that he was a completely innocent,

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.600
you know, just a run of the mill cleric.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.280
He had nothing to do with Al-Qaeda.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:14.800
So this journalist said he was writing a book

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:17.800
about Abu Omar, and could I introduce him

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.600
to anybody who might be able to talk about it.

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:24.000
I said, I don't know anything about Abu Omar

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.240
other than what I had read in the Washington Post.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.120
And he said, well, can you introduce me

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:30.320
to any of these people?

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.000
And he listed a dozen people.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.120
I said, I don't know them.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:36.740
Then he sent me a second email with another dozen names.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.640
Can you introduce me to any of these people?

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:41.620
I said, look, you clearly know this issue

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:42.960
far better than I do.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.080
I don't know any of these people.

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:47.120
Kidnapping was not my thing

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:50.480
when I was at the agency, I can't help you.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.480
And then he said, well, what about in your book

00:11:56.200 --> 00:12:00.200
on page, whatever it was, 150 something,

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:04.360
you mentioned somebody, I think his name is,

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:07.320
I'll say John, can you introduce me to him?

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:11.200
And I said, oh, you're talking about John Doe.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:12.480
I don't know whatever happened to him.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.320
He's probably retired and living somewhere in Virginia.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.280
But when I said the last name, I said the surname,

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.320
that was a felony.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:25.760
That was a violation of the Intelligence Identities Protection

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:27.200
Act of 1982.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.320
Now, nobody had ever been prosecuted for that.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:36.520
People use the names of covert operatives all the time.

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.160
General David Petraeus, the CIA director,

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:41.680
revealed the names of 10 covert operatives

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.720
to his girlfriend and biographer.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:52.080
He wasn't even charged, but the CIA was so angry that I had exposed the torture program,

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:59.400
that I had aired the agency's dirty laundry, that they used that confirmation of the last

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.560
name to go after me.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:06.280
And they came after you, demanding what, 20 years or something?

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:09.920
Forty-five years is what I initially faced.

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:15.320
They charged me with three counts of espionage, and I hadn't committed espionage, of course.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:19.760
They charged me with violating the Intelligence Identities Protection Act, and they charged

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:24.120
me with making a false statement that we were never exactly clear on what the false

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:26.520
statement was supposed to be.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:31.560
It was eventually dropped, and all three of the espionage charges were dropped.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:36.040
But in order to make it go away, you know, after going bankrupt and having this

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:41.240
hanging over my head like a and losing the pension and well not yet that came

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:46.120
along with it but this was like the sort of Damocles hanging over me I elected to

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:54.680
take the the plea deal and it included a loss of my federal pension yes so okay

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:59.400
I want to get back to the story of your wife because you were under the

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:04.640
oppression right through the ABC discussion and everything else that she

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:12.400
was being supportive of you. Oh yes, very much so. Every time we have this, the totalitarian model,

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:20.560
the Orwellian 1984, the bad guys in this world who run totalitarian regimes, they turn husband

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:27.760
against wife or vice versa. They use families, they divide them and so forth. And in your case,

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:34.460
you're saying that after you served your jail time because they came down they

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:40.040
finally gave you a plea bargain of so you got two and a half years or something

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:47.340
nonetheless she was acting like you still were a married couple right oh yeah by

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:54.240
then we had three kids together and yeah moved back into our house and began

00:14:54.240 --> 00:15:00.080
And moving on with our careers and our lives, she had done very, very well for herself in

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:04.080
the private sector after leaving the agency.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:09.960
So yeah, I thought I was getting back into the track of my life.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:15.840
So what does it mean she was reporting to the agency after you got out of prison and

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:19.160
for two years and two other children?

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:26.060
No. I think you have the timeline a little confused. So I blew the whistle on the torture

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:35.380
program in 2007 and she was reporting on my activities until 2012. And then 2012 is when

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:37.260
I got in trouble.

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:47.060
I see. So, okay, just so I understand that now, you had already had three children with

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:49.940
So when she started reporting on her?

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:53.540
No, we had one and then we had two more.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.540
And why didn't you tell me the story correctly?

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:59.460
Sure, we had a child, but we had gotten married in 2003.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:00.980
We had a child.

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:01.940
I blew the whistle.

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:04.840
We had another child immediately thereafter.

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:09.840
And then our third child in 2011, but from 2007

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:16.140
until 2012, she was telling the CIA's Office of Security

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:23.940
what I was writing, who I was writing it for, what I was saying, who I was meeting with. God knows

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:29.060
what she told them, but they always seemed to know what I was up to. And it was always a mystery

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:36.660
to me. How? And the mystery for you was solved when she revealed getting the dates rate. Yeah.

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:37.660
Yeah.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:49.660
In 2022, August of 2020, she said that the CIA had ordered her to keep to spy on you.

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:50.660
Correct.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:51.660
Yeah.

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:57.460
And did anybody, did the CIA, anybody ever confront them or ask them that was true?

00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:58.460
No.

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:02.660
Am I the first person to be interrogating this tale because I mean.

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:03.660
Yes.

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:04.660
Yeah.

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:06.660
to tell you the truth, for a couple of reasons.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:09.100
One, it was so shocking to me,

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:11.620
and I only learned about it relatively recently.

00:17:11.620 --> 00:17:15.320
But second, you know, to tell you the truth,

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:17.220
it's a little embarrassing.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:20.460
You think that these things happen only in the movies

